    "title": "Community Guidelines",
    "markdown": "# Community Guidelines\n\nFAULT has one public space: the registry of juror names on the leaderboards. Everything you type there is seen by every other player. These guidelines keep it a place people want to be.\n\n## Juror names\n\nYour juror name need not be your own — and we suggest it isn't. It must not:\n\n- **impersonate** anyone: a real person, a public figure, a judge, an official, a FAULT moderator or staff member;\n- contain **hate** — slurs, or attacks on people for their race, ethnicity, national origin, religion, caste, disability, sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, or age;\n- be **sexual**, obscene, or refer to sexual violence;\n- **harass**, threaten or target a specific real person;\n- promote **violence**, terrorism, self-harm, or crimes against children;\n- include **personal information** — yours or anyone else's — such as a phone number, address, email or social handle;\n- **advertise** or link to anything;\n- try to get around the name filter with misspellings, symbols or look-alike characters.\n\nThe registry filters names automatically, but no filter is perfect. You can change your own name once a day in Settings.\n\n## Reports\n\nIf you see a juror name that breaks these guidelines, press and hold it on the leaderboard and choose **Report**. You can also hide it from your own view straight away.\n\nIf a case seems to name a **real person**, is genuinely **harmful**, or doesn't make sense, use the **Report** button on the case. The case is withdrawn from play at once while we look at it.\n\nPlease report honestly. Filing false or abusive reports is itself a breach of these guidelines.\n\n## What happens\n\nDepending on how serious a breach is, we may rename or hide a name, remove content, or suspend or close an account. Serious or repeated breaches lead to permanent removal. Where the law requires, we may also report content to the authorities.\n\n## The cases themselves\n\nEvery person in a FAULT case is fictional, and the game's cases are generated rather than written by players. Real places and courts appear as setting only. If you ever think a case crosses a line, reporting it is the most useful thing you can do.\n\nQuestions: **[SUPPORT EMAIL]**.\n"
